## Is It Worth Removing BIAB at Home, or Booking a Salon Removal?

For most people with a straightforward set, home removal is worth doing yourself. It costs the price of acetone and cotton pads you likely already own, against a salon removal fee that typically runs <strong>£12 to £28</strong> on its own, separate from any new set applied afterward. Weighed against the <a href='/blog/biab-cost/biab-cost-guide'>full BIAB cost breakdown</a>, paying that fee purely to have a set taken off, with no new manicure booked, rarely makes sense when the DIY version takes under an hour.

A salon visit earns its cost back when the set is unusually thick, when you have tried the home method and a nail genuinely will not budge after two attempts, or when your nails already feel compromised and you would rather a professional judge how much filing is safe. Outside those situations, the home method described above handles a standard set reliably.

<hr className="my-8 border-gray-200" />

Removing a set is rarely the end goal anyway. Most people who master this process end up using it less over time, not more, because the smarter long-term move is infilling rather than stripping a set back to bare nail every few weeks. A well-timed infill protects the nail plate from the repeated soak-strip-recover cycle that full removals put it through, and it keeps the set looking fresh without ever starting from zero. Once you have the removal method down, treat it as a fallback for when you want a genuine break, and let <a href='/blog/biab-cost/biab-infill-guide'>what a BIAB infill involves and costs</a> do the heavy lifting the rest of the time.
